@meta
id: 24-14
title: Sort by the number after a pattern
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: ex-sort, ex-move
par: 32
minutes: 5
vscode: nothing built in; a spreadsheet, probably
open: errors.ts

@brief
The error map should be ordered by status code, lowest first, so the
4xx codes read as a group above the 5xx ones. Lines 2 to 8 need
sorting. The two 404s keep their current relative order. The first and
last lines of the file do not move.

Plain `:sort` would order by the key name. `:sort n` sorts by the first
number it finds on each line, which is nearly right, except that two of
the keys have digits in them and `n` would sort those on the wrong
number. So tell it where to look. `:sort n /pattern/` skips past the
match and sorts on the first number after it.

@keys
| `:2,8sort n /statusCode/` | sort lines 2 to 8 by the number after `statusCode` |
| `:sort n` | sort by the first number in each line |
| `:sort!` | sort in reverse |
| `:sort /pat/` | sort by what comes after the match |

@hints
Range, `sort`, the numeric flag, then a pattern to skip past.
--
Look at `GATEWAY_V2_TIMEOUT` and `TIER_3_REQUIRED`. Without the
pattern, `n` sorts those on `2` and `3`. The pattern only has to match
something that sits before the code on every line.
--
`:2,8sort n /statusCode/` and Enter, `:wq`.

@solution
    :2,8sort n /statusCode/<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-eight keystrokes.

The pattern argument to `sort` is the piece most people never learn
and it changes what the command is for. `:sort /pat/` sorts on the text
*after* the match, so `:sort /,/` sorts a CSV by its second column and
`:sort /: /` sorts a YAML block by value rather than by key. Add `n` and
the comparison is numeric, so `10` comes after `9` rather than before
it. Add `!` and the order flips; `:2,8sort! n /statusCode/` would have
put the 5xx codes at the top.

`sort` is stable: lines that compare equal stay in the order they were
in. That is why the two 404s did not swap. It is also why `:sort` can
be run twice with different keys to get a secondary sort, least
significant key first.

@start errors.ts
export const errors = {
  REFUND_FAILED: { statusCode: 502, sentry: true },
  ORDER_NOT_FOUND: { statusCode: 404, sentry: false },
  GATEWAY_V2_TIMEOUT: { statusCode: 504, sentry: true },
  ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED: { statusCode: 409, sentry: false },
  CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND: { statusCode: 404, sentry: false },
  TIER_3_REQUIRED: { statusCode: 403, sentry: false },
  PAYMENT_DECLINED: { statusCode: 402, sentry: false },
};

@expected errors.ts
export const errors = {
  PAYMENT_DECLINED: { statusCode: 402, sentry: false },
  TIER_3_REQUIRED: { statusCode: 403, sentry: false },
  ORDER_NOT_FOUND: { statusCode: 404, sentry: false },
  CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND: { statusCode: 404, sentry: false },
  ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED: { statusCode: 409, sentry: false },
  REFUND_FAILED: { statusCode: 502, sentry: true },
  GATEWAY_V2_TIMEOUT: { statusCode: 504, sentry: true },
};
